---

<ProjectLayout {...props}>
	<h2 id="why-it-exists">Why it exists</h2>
	<p>
		Arabic historical manuscripts are an underserved corner of OCR. Modern
		Arabic OCR works reasonably well on clean printed text; on
		century-or-older manuscripts it falls apart. Documents are degraded,
		calligraphic styles vary wildly, diacritics are inconsistent, and labelled
		training data barely exists. Commercial OCR engines either skip Arabic
		historical content entirely or produce output that's worse than nothing.
	</p>
	<p>
		This is my senior graduation project at Prince Sultan University. The goal
		is a system that meaningfully reduces character error rate on Arabic
		manuscript pages, with a methodology that's reproducible and a dataset
		that's reusable beyond this project.
	</p>

	<h2 id="what-i-built">What I built</h2>
	<p>
		An end-to-end pipeline: collected and annotated dataset → preprocessing
		stack (binarization, denoising, skew correction, line segmentation) → text
		recognition model → evaluation framework. Each stage is swappable, so the
		ablations are real ablations rather than rerunning a black box with
		different inputs.
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>A hybrid binarization scheme that combines Otsu, Sauvola, and Niblack adaptively</li>
		<li>An attention mechanism tuned for Arabic connected script</li>
		<li>A linguistic post-processor that corrects context-plausible errors</li>
		<li>A reusable evaluation harness reporting CER, WER, F1 at character and word level</li>
	</ul>

	<h2 id="methodology">Methodology</h2>

	<h3>Dataset</h3>
	<p>
		Pulled from digital archives, scanned library collections, and synthetic
		augmentation. Annotation is line-level transcription with manuscript
		category metadata (era, condition, calligraphic style). Stored in
		MongoDB so I can slice evaluation by category later.
	</p>

	<h3>Preprocessing</h3>
	<p>Comprehensive pipeline before recognition:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>Adaptive binarization: Otsu, Sauvola, Niblack, plus a hybrid combiner</li>
		<li>Noise reduction and stain filtering</li>
		<li>Skew correction and normalization</li>
		<li>Text-line segmentation with margin-aware heuristics</li>
		<li>Image enhancement specific to Arabic script characteristics</li>
	</ul>

	<h3>Models</h3>
	<p>Three architectures compared on the same preprocessing pipeline:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>CNN-RNN hybrid with attention: strong baseline</li>
		<li>Transformer-based recognizer: best on clean text, struggles on degraded</li>
		<li>Custom architecture tuned for Arabic ligatures and diacritics: best overall</li>
	</ul>

	<h2 id="evaluation">Evaluation</h2>
	<p>
		Reported metrics: Character Error Rate (CER), Word Error Rate (WER),
		precision/recall/F1 at character and word level, plus processing time.
		Each model is evaluated on three slices: clean printed Arabic (control),
		modern handwritten Arabic, and historical manuscript pages. The slice
		split matters: averaging across all three hides the fact that the
		degraded-historical slice is where most of the engineering work pays off.
	</p>
	<p>
		Baselines: Tesseract (Arabic), Google Cloud Vision, Azure Read API. The
		−30% CER number compares the custom architecture against the strongest
		commercial baseline on the historical slice; on clean printed text the
		commercial systems are competitive or better, which is fine. That's not
		the problem this project is solving.
	</p>

	<h2 id="hard-parts">Hard parts</h2>
	<p>
		<strong>Annotation cost.</strong> Manuscript transcription requires
		expertise; I can do some, but consistent annotation at scale needs help.
		Synthetic data fills gaps but doesn't substitute for the long tail of
		real-world degradation patterns.
	</p>
	<p>
		<strong>Calligraphic variation.</strong> A model that nails one style
		(Naskh) often regresses on another (Diwani, Thuluth). Ended up with a
		style-conditioning input that improves the worst case without hurting the
		best.
	</p>
	<p>
		<strong>Evaluation honesty.</strong> Easy to overfit to the test set when
		you're iterating fast. Locked a holdout slice early and only ran it at
		decision points.
	</p>

	<h2 id="results">Results</h2>
	<p>
		On the historical-manuscript evaluation slice, the custom architecture
		paired with the hybrid preprocessing pipeline reduces CER by roughly 30%
		relative to the strongest commercial baseline. The improvement comes from
		preprocessing more than from model choice. Once the binarization is
		right, all three model architectures get noticeably better.
	</p>
	<p>
		Beyond the headline number: the framework is what I'd reuse. Swapping in a
		new model takes a couple of days, not weeks, because the dataset
		organization, preprocessing options, and evaluation harness are all
		already there.
	</p>

	<h2 id="limitations">Limitations & future work</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Dataset breadth: more historical periods, more calligraphic styles, more annotators</li>
		<li>Self-supervised pretraining on unlabelled scans: there's a lot of unlabelled data sitting in archives</li>
		<li>Few-shot adaptation for rare manuscript types instead of training from scratch each time</li>
		<li>Tighter integration with the Makhtotah Platform for true end-to-end manuscript digitization</li>
	</ul>

	<h2 id="what-i-learned">What I learned</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Preprocessing is where the wins live for degraded-document OCR. Model architecture matters less than I expected.</li>
		<li>An evaluation harness that splits by document category is worth building even before the first model. Aggregate metrics lie.</li>
		<li>Synthetic data is great for rare characters, mediocre for rare degradation patterns. You can't simulate what aging actually does to ink.</li>
		<li>Lock the holdout early. Future-me will thank present-me.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2 id="acknowledgments">Acknowledgments</h2>
	<p>
		Project supervised by faculty at Prince Sultan University. Thanks to the
		colleagues who reviewed annotation samples for consistency, and to the
		archives whose scanned collections made the dataset possible.
	</p>
</ProjectLayout>
